      "writtenOn": "2023-04-20T17:19:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Using `2500` directly makes it very specific to a particular fan\u0027s capabilities.\n\nInstead of using specific RPM values, can this instead be expressed with duty values? For example, with a fan that is capable of running at 4800 RPM (`rpm_max`, 100% duty), an RPM change of `2500` would be the same as a duty change of \u003e52%. So, rather than checking `ABS(rpm_diff) \u003e\u003d 2500`, calculate the target duty difference, and place bounds around how much it can actually change.",
      "revId": "f801fdb0eff86c769ef817bccd932bb8fbb25498",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c017d284_7df4de50",
        "filename": "zephyr/program/skyrim/winterhold/src/thermal.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 258,
      "author": {
        "id": 1395927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-20T17:43:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes this is true, though fix is applied as Whiterun only and in fact address it\u0027s issues with dynamic pressure build-up and overshoot during the ramp up. Again the PID as final fix would be configurable and would allow to use proportional an derivative coefficients. We plan those can be defined per each fan and per board separately.\n\nHere we are trying to be as close to the original code as possible to not introduce unwanted behaviors for AU release. The rpm_max for instance is currently used to limit the max noise level. If ODM will change those and we use them for calculating the PWM step diffs for integration, those will influence on ramp up scenario.\nIn contrast, once we will implement PID the dynamic behavior can be easily controlled by Kp, Ki, Kd in separation from other existing RPM limitation params which purpose is currently used by ODM for limiting the max noise.",
      "parentUuid": "193cef00_7fb35141",
      "revId": "f801fdb0eff86c769ef817bccd932bb8fbb25498",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e801bd7d_18148dc8",
        "filename": "zephyr/program/skyrim/winterhold/src/thermal.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 258,
      "author": {
        "id": 1565377
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-20T17:53:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "```\n For example, with a fan that is capable of running at 4800 RPM (rpm_max, 100% duty), an RPM change of 2500 would be the same as a duty change of \u003e52%. So, rather than checking ABS(rpm_diff) \u003e\u003d 2500, calculate the target duty difference, and place bounds around how much it can actually change.\n\n```\n\nThe problem here is that the relation between fan duty and RPM change is non-linear. The values under those if-s have found experimentally, after quite a lot of fine tuning.\nI agree that this is not the ideal solution, though as Radosław mentioned, at some point we indent to fix this properly using PID.",
      "parentUuid": "c017d284_7df4de50",
      "revId": "f801fdb0eff86c769ef817bccd932bb8fbb25498",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2d7a9f2b_82c15603",
        "filename": "zephyr/program/skyrim/winterhold/src/thermal.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 258,
      "author": {
        "id": 1297197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-20T20:16:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The problem here is that the relation between fan duty and RPM change is non-linear.\n\nIt appears this function is mixing units then. We are making calculations based on fan RPM, but setting the fan duty. How do we ever expect to hit the target RPM without knowing the duty-\u003eRPM translation, when we\u0027re modifying the fan speed using duty?\n\nCan we get the duty-\u003eRPM translation function?",
      "parentUuid": "e801bd7d_18148dc8",
      "revId": "f801fdb0eff86c769ef817bccd932bb8fbb25498",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5a580e88_9f2aaf22",
        "filename": "zephyr/program/skyrim/winterhold/src/thermal.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 258,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-20T20:43:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Please document that these RPM values were determined empirically and are specific to the Whiterun fan.",
      "parentUuid": "e801bd7d_18148dc8",
      "revId": "f801fdb0eff86c769ef817bccd932bb8fbb25498",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "01755746_3560c9bb",
        "filename": "zephyr/program/skyrim/winterhold/src/thermal.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 258,
      "author": {
        "id": 1565377
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-21T11:39:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "```\nIt appears this function is mixing units then. We are making calculations based on fan RPM, but setting the fan duty. How do we ever expect to hit the target RPM without knowing the duty-\u003eRPM translation, when we\u0027re modifying the fan speed using duty?\n\nCan we get the duty-\u003eRPM translation function?\n\n```\n\nOh, I see what you mean.\nInstead of calculating the delta of fan_duty based on RPM difference we could get set the duty based on target RPM. We can do that since the relation between duty and RPM can be found experimentally.\n\nFrankly I like your solution more, the problem is that we\u0027re out of time.\nMaking such a change would require to re-do the fine tuning from scratch.\n\nAlso the current approach should work better in case ODM uses a different fan, with similar characteristics.",
      "parentUuid": "5a580e88_9f2aaf22",
      "revId": "f801fdb0eff86c769ef817bccd932bb8fbb25498",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "12b9541a_93655cc7",
        "filename": "zephyr/program/skyrim/winterhold/src/thermal.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 258,
      "author": {
        "id": 1395927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-21T13:58:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e How do we ever expect to hit the target RPM without knowing the duty-\u003eRPM translation,\n\nThe \"control theory\" is a wide field. The code resembles something which I would call \"integration controller\" (though my brain hurts when I write this). For such closed loop feedback controllers the error signal and control signals are usually in different dimensions as the whole point is to minimize the error within the required reaction time. This type of controller has limited possibilities for coping with non linearity (especially dynamic effects) which lead to overshoots and oscillations, but this is a well proven theory in the control theory field. The implementation though is another story and we wrote long explanation in the associated bug why it is broken and how to fix it. It is something we want to use as a quick fix as it proff to work for some time now and we need this fix before AU. Though we definitely plan to implement a proper PID to replace it.\n\n\u003e Can we get the duty-\u003eRPM translation function?\n\nFrom the control theory it is what is called \"linear open loop\". It can be done but usually non-linear dynamic feedback loops is used to accommodate dynamic properties of the system. The feedback loops have nice property that a single set of parameters is able to work with quite wide rage of devices which share the same properties (as the fan in a box in this case). A prominent and most typical example is PID which we plan ot implement as long term solution.\nIf you want to get more information why such solution is suggested by us and how it should be coded, I\u0027m happy to share the coming design document where we will try to elaborate more over this and similar questions.\nBut again, this patch is just short term. Long term solution is in design phase and address also other issues we found.",
      "parentUuid": "01755746_3560c9bb",
      "revId": "f801fdb0eff86c769ef817bccd932bb8fbb25498",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
